      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Dynamic Light Scattering (DLS)",
      "why": "Dynamic light scattering measures the hydrodynamic diameter and polydispersity index (PDI) of sub-micron vesicles like liposomes directly in your aqueous buffer without drying the sample. Note that DLS is heavily weighted toward larger scattering particles, so aggregates or dust can skew the distribution and samples should be appropriately diluted to avoid multiple scattering.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Compatible sizing cuvette (e.g., disposable polystyrene, low-volume, or quartz cuvette suitable for aqueous buffer)",
        "Folded capillary cell if also measuring zeta potential"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ11",
      "role": "supplementary",
      "technique": "Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM)",
      "why": "TEM can provide direct visual confirmation of liposome size and morphology, though standard negative-stain TEM requires drying the sample on a grid in vacuum, which can cause shrinkage or structural artifacts rather than measuring them directly in buffer.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "TEM grids and negative staining reagents (e.g., uranyl acetate or phosphotungstic acid)",
        "Cryo-transfer holder and vitrification equipment (if cryo-TEM is desired to preserve native hydrated structure)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [
    {
      "technique": "Nanoparticle Tracking Analysis (NTA)",
      "why_useful": "NTA tracks individual liposomes in liquid suspension to provide a number-weighted size distribution and particle concentration, which resolves multimodal or polydisperse populations better than ensemble DLS measurements."
    },
    {
      "technique": "Tunable Resistive Pulse Sensing (TRPS)",
      "why_useful": "TRPS measures liposomes particle-by-particle in conductive buffer to give high-resolution size distribution and concentration data."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "You can measure the hydrodynamic size distribution and polydispersity index of your liposomes directly in buffer using Dynamic Light Scattering (DLS) on the Malvern Zetasizer Pro (EQ20). Keep in mind that DLS is an ensemble technique sensitive to larger aggregates, so for direct visualization of individual vesicle morphology you could supplement DLS with transmission electron microscopy (EQ11), though standard TEM requires drying the sample."
}

