        "Conductive sample stubs"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [
    {
      "technique": "Dedicated Cryo-Transmission Electron Microscopy (Cryo-TEM) with vitrification system",
      "why_useful": "Cryo-TEM is the gold standard for imaging lipid nanoparticles because rapid plunge-freezing preserves their native hydrated shape, internal lipid lamellarity, and core structure without the collapse or distortion caused by drying in standard TEM."
    },
    {
      "technique": "Nanoparticle Tracking Analysis (NTA)",
      "why_useful": "NTA tracks individual nanoparticles in liquid suspension to provide number-based size distributions and particle concentration alongside hydrodynamic size."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "Lipid nanoparticles are too small to be resolved with visible light on a stereomicroscope. To see both their size and shape, use the transmission electron microscope (EQ11), keeping in mind that soft lipid particles typically require negative staining and may deform during drying unless cryo-preparation is available. Pair this with the Zetasizer Pro (EQ20) to accurately measure their hydrodynamic size distribution in liquid suspension."
}

{
